{"statement": "Treating machine learning training as functional indexing is a legal fiction designed to launder uncompensated appropriation of human creative labor into corporate capital. Search engine caching stores transient pointers to help users find publicly displayed material. Generative training encodes expressive patterns from millions of copyrighted books, images, and news articles into proprietary weight matrices owned by a handful of firms. Conflating a temporary transmission cache with permanent statistical encodement ignores the physical reality of how these models are built.

Enforcing data provenance and creator compensation is the only way to establish accountability in an industry built on mass scraping. When publishers block web crawlers via robots.txt protocols, firms simply route through intermediary scrapers like Common Crawl or purchase datasets from brokers like LDC to bypass consent. Claiming that training data audits are technically infeasible for models with billions of parameters does not excuse unauthorized extraction. Congress required pharmaceutical companies to prove the origin of their chemical compounds and financial institutions to trace the provenance of securities. Lawmakers can apply the exact same transparency mandate to training corpora without breaking open-source software.

The enforcement mechanism sits within the U.S. Copyright Office and the Federal Trade Commission, using mandatory public disclosure rules and algorithmic disgorgement to penalize unlawful scraping. The political coalition backing this consists of independent authors, photojournalists, labor unions, and regional publishers who watched their advertising revenue and licensing markets evaporate between 2012 and 2024. The opposition relies on well-funded technology lobbyists warning of regulatory capture and foreign competition. Those arguments collapse the moment someone asks who pays the human workers labeling the data and who collects the billions in venture capital profits. Historical precedent exists in the Digital Millennium Copyright Act of 1998, which created strict notice-and-takedown frameworks for digital platforms. Policymakers can apply that exact ledger logic to training datasets today.", "turn_symbols": [{"symbol": "🗄️", "tooltip": "A temporary warehouse shelf is not a factory that grinds down raw materials into a permanent product."}, {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Tracing the source of raw inputs is standard practice in every regulated supply chain from food to finance."}], "claim_sketches": [{"claim": "Treating machine learning training as functional indexing is a legal fiction designed to launder uncompensated appropriation of human creative labor into corporate capital.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}, {"claim": "Enforcing data provenance and creator compensation is the only way to establish accountability in an industry built on mass scraping.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}]}

