  "statement": "Treating neural network training as copyright infringement creates an artificial legal tollbooth that criminalizes how all intelligence learns. Skeptics argue that processing billions of tokens without individual licensing payments strips human creators of economic agency. That argument correctly identifies the friction of market transition, but it treats mathematical abstraction like static piracy. Deep learning models do not store verbatim copies of expressive works in search engine caches or database tables. Frontier models compress human language and visual patterns into billions of floating-point numbers across high-dimensional vector spaces. A neural network retains general operational logic rather than static expressive copies, mirroring how human readers synthesize knowledge from public books without paying every author a toll.\n\nEnforcing mandatory licensing regimes for data ingestion hands absolute market veto power to legacy media incumbents while crushing decentralized open-source development. When the European Union enacted strict compliance burdens under the Artificial Intelligence Act in 2024, startup formation rates for foundational models dropped by forty percent within six months. Venture capital fled to jurisdictions with permissive text and data mining exceptions. The enforcement mechanism rests on civil litigation and statutory damages up to one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per infringed work under Title 17 of the United States Code. That liability structure makes permissionless innovation illegal for anyone outside the Fortune 500. A two-person garage startup cannot afford the legal discovery costs to clear ten million copyrighted web pages before running a single training run.\n\nDelaying capability scaling out of hypothetical fear carries a direct human body count that policy reports routinely ignore. Medical research platforms like AlphaFold process structural protein data across millions of public sequences to map biological machinery in minutes instead of decades. Every month that regulatory friction delays model training and deployment is a month where automated antibiotic discovery stalls and preventable diseases claim thousands of lives. Policymakers must reject the false choice between creator compensation and human progress by keeping public data ingestion open and unrestricted. Congress should enact statutory safe harbors for non-expressive machine learning ingestion, following the precedent established in Authors Guild v. Google regarding transformative digital preservation. If lawmakers choose to freeze development to protect legacy business models, they are explicitly choosing to let people suffer from delays in medical cures they could have automated.",
